  "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}-0(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$",
